From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: vote the core clock for the full pixel clock on video-mode interfaces

_dpu_core_perf_calc_clk() derives the core clock from the active pixel rate
(vtotal * hdisplay * vrefresh), 1.05x. A video-mode interface scans out on
the sink's timing, so the DPU has to keep up with the full pixel clock. On
the ROG5 side port with only DP lit (1920x1080@60, pixel clock 148.5 MHz),
the vote was 136 MHz (150 MHz OPP) and INTF_0 underran on every frame (the
monitor showed the underflow colour); while the DSI command-mode panel was
also on, its vote kept the core at 345 MHz and hid this. With the vote at
156 MHz (200 MHz OPP): 0 underruns in 385 frames.

Use the mode's pixel clock as the floor when a video-mode encoder drives
the CRTC.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_core_perf.c	2026-09-29 14:25:58.862006401 +0200
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_core_perf.c	2026-09-29 14:25:58.875426875 +0200
@@ -118,6 +118,24 @@
 
 	mode_clk = (u64)mode->vtotal * mode->hdisplay * drm_mode_vrefresh(mode);
 
+	/*
+	 * A video-mode interface (DP) scans out on the sink's timing: the DPU
+	 * has to keep up with the full pixel clock, blanking included. The
+	 * active-pixel rate alone voted 136 MHz for 1080p60 (pixel clock
+	 * 148.5 MHz) and INTF_0 underran on every frame on the ROG5 side port
+	 * once the panel (whose command mode kept the clock at 345 MHz) was off.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct drm_encoder *encoder;
+
+		drm_for_each_encoder_mask(encoder, crtc->dev, state->encoder_mask) {
+			if (dpu_encoder_get_intf_mode(encoder) == INTF_MODE_VIDEO) {
+				mode_clk = max_t(u64, mode_clk, (u64)mode->clock * 1000);
+				break;
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (dpu_cmd_mode_clk_pct > 100) {
 		struct drm_encoder *encoder;
 
